      "text": "We can represent four consecutive integers as (x), (x + 1), (x + 2), and (x + 3).\n\nHence, (x) multiplied by ((x + 1)), ((x + 2)), and ((x + 3)) gives the product of 840.\n\nThis implies that,\n[x^4 + 6x^3 + 11x^2 + 6x] = 840\n\n[x^3 (x + 6) + 1 (x + 6)] = 840\n\nLet's denote x + 6 as p. Thus, our equation simplifies further to:\n\n[x^3p] = 840\n[x^3] * [p] = 840\n\nThe integer cubes we consider (from 1 to 7) are 1, 8, 27, and 64. We go through the list of divisors of 840, which are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 10, 12, 14, 20, 21, 28, 35, 42, 56, 60, 70, 84, 105, 140, 168, 210, 280, 420, and 840.\n\n1. First, (x + 6) must equal 7, because all other cubes are too small. Solving (x + 6 = 7) gives us (x = 1). Therefore, the four integers are 1, 7, 8, and 9.\n\n2. If (x + 6 = 8), this gives (x = 2), the four integers would be 2, 8, 9, and 10.\n\n3. For (x + 6 = 9), (x = 3) results in four integers as 3, 9, 10, and 11.\n\n4. If (x + 6 = 10), then (x = 4), leading to integers 4, 10, 11, and 12.\n\n5. Additionally, (x + 6 = 12) gives us (x = 6), resulting in integers 6, 12, 13, and 14.\n\nThus, the integers could be combinations of the above sets, but the largest must be determined from the calculated sets. The largest integer is 14.\n\nThe answer is 14.",
